// ── The semantic content IR ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `Content` is the target-neutral document vocabulary: one variant per
// document concept, no generic container, and no escape into markup.
// It is the layer every backend (HTML, PDF, Markdown) is meant
// to consume, in place of each one reverse-engineering semantics out of
// `Html` — a heading whose level rides in a CSS class, a
// callout re-implemented three times, a walker that ends in `_ => {}`.
//
// Three rules the declaration below obeys, and why:
//
//   1. **Closed.** There is no `Html { }` / `Raw { }` variant and no
//      generic `Container { role, children }`. A concept that is not in
//      this union is not page content: a user keeps bespoke *drawings*
//      (`Svg`, via `Drawing`) and gives up bespoke page
//      *markup*. Adding a concept means adding a variant — which every
//      backend then has to match, by construction.
//   2. **One variant per concept.** A `role: symbol` on a shared
//      container is a stringly-typed key each backend interprets or
//      ignores; that is the very divergence this layer exists to end.
//   3. **Semantics in fields, never in strings.** `Heading.level` is a
//      number, not `class: ["heading-2"]`; `Callout.kind` is a symbol
//      from a declared vocabulary, not a class name matched by hand.
//
// `class` survives as a *style hint* only (the `class` system is read by
// the non-HTML backends too, so it is not HTML chrome) and `id` as an
// anchor target. Neither may carry meaning a backend has to parse back
// out — that is exactly the mistake being deleted.
//
// This file is the source of truth for the Rust `Content` enum: the
// wdoc build script parses it and generates `crates/wcl_wdoc/src/`'s
// `content` module (the enum, the supporting types, and the
// `Value` → `Content` conversion). Edit the union, not the Rust.
//
// `callout` was the first block routed through it (`callout.wcl`); the
// markup-using blocks followed, and the final payload-shaped page blocks
// (`math`, `video`, `sequence_diagram`, `state_diagram`) complete the
// classification below. All three backends read these nodes from this
// declaration and match the union exhaustively.
//
// ── Page-content classification ───────────────────────────────────
//
// The test is mechanical: a block whose lowering can carry a fixed payload
// becomes a `Content` producer; a block that needs the authored block
// subtree (or renderer-only state that no IR payload can hold) is `@native`.
// This is the complete inventory of the 33 stdlib `ContentBlock` types.
//
// Content producers (16):
//
//   block(s)                       test and reason
//   ─────────────────────────────  ────────────────────────────────────────
//   callout                        fixed heading/kind/icon + authored body prose
//   chapter_header                 fixed title/kicker/meta payload
//   code                           fixed source/language/filename payload
//   footnotes                      child records resolve to fixed note records
//   h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6         fixed level/text payload
//   math                           fixed LaTeX/display payload
//   p, text                        fixed prose payload (`text` joins span records)
//   sequence_diagram               child records lower completely to `Drawing.shapes`
//   state_diagram                  child records lower completely to `Drawing.shapes`
//   video                          fixed source/poster/title/size payload
//
// Native blocks (17):
//
//   block(s)                       test and reason
//   ─────────────────────────────  ────────────────────────────────────────
//   column, fragment, notes        need arbitrary child `ContentBlock` subtrees
//   list                           needs nested `li`/`list` block structure
//   terminal                       needs its terminal-widget subtree and Rust grid
//   diagram                        needs its arbitrary `SvgBlock` subtree/layout
//   demo, markdown_source          need child source text and rendered subtrees
//   wdoc_content, wdoc_repeater,
//   wdoc_instance                  need component/repeater expansion context
//   partial, collect, project       need cross-tree lookup, expansion and cycle guards
//   image                          one type is both page content and a native SVG shape;
//                                  asset metadata/dual placement cannot declare one lower
//   table                          pipe rows live in authored block syntax, not its value
//   file                           shipping is an output side effect and PDF is uncovered
//
// A native block can still represent a document concept; `@native` says why
// its *authored value* cannot cross this IR seam. The corresponding union
// variants remain available to user lowerings and to future producers.

namespace wdoc

// The kind of admonition a `Callout` is, replacing today's
// match-on-a-class-name (`class = ["warning"]`). Each backend maps the
// symbol to its own accent / icon / prefix.
symbol_set CalloutKind { note info tip warning error success }

// One item of a `List`. `text` is the item's own inline prose (run
// through the inline-pattern engine by the backend, like every other
// prose field here); `blocks` carries any content nested underneath it
// — a sub-`List`, a `Code` sample, a `Paragraph`.
type ContentListItem {
  @doc("The item's own prose. Inline patterns apply.")
  text:   utf8
  @doc("Content nested under this item (a sub-list, a code sample, …).")
  blocks: list<Content>?
}

// One line of a `Toc`. `depth` is the nesting level (0 = top), `target`
// the page/anchor the entry links to (absent for a grouping heading)
// and `number` the resolved section number, if the site numbers its
// chapters.
type ContentTocEntry {
  @doc("Nesting level; 0 is a top-level entry.")
  depth:  u8
  @doc("The entry's title text.")
  title:  utf8
  @doc("Page or anchor this entry links to; absent for a grouping heading.")
  target: utf8?
  @doc("Resolved section number (e.g. `2.1`), when the site numbers chapters.")
  number: utf8?
}

// One resolved footnote: the marker printed at both ends (`1`, `a`, …)
// and the note's prose.
type ContentFootnote {
  @doc("The marker printed at the reference and beside the note.")
  marker: utf8
  @doc("The note's prose. Inline patterns apply.")
  text:   utf8
}

union Content {
  // ── Prose ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  @doc("A section heading. `level` is 1–6 — a number, never a CSS class.")
  Heading   { level: u8 text: utf8 id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("A body paragraph. `text` runs through the inline-pattern engine.")
  Paragraph { text: utf8 id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("A bullet or numbered list; nesting lives in each item's `blocks`.")
  List      { items: list<ContentListItem> style: ListStyle? start: u32? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("A data table: an optional heading row plus body rows of cell prose.")
  Table     { rows: list<list<utf8>> header: list<utf8>? caption: utf8? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("A code listing. Highlighting is the backend's job; `filename` is the header a listing may carry. Exactly one of `source` (the text) and `source_file` (a path read at build time, optionally narrowed by `anchor` or `lines`) is authored; the include pass resolves the file into `source` before any backend sees the node.")
  Code      {
    source: utf8? source_file: utf8? anchor: utf8? lines: utf8? dedent: bool?
    language: identifier? filename: utf8? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>?
  }
  @doc("An admonition: a typed accent, a heading, and nested body content.")
  Callout   { kind: CalloutKind? heading: utf8 body: list<Content> icon: utf8? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("Side-by-side content columns; each column is its own content list.")
  Columns   { columns: list<list<Content>> id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }

  // ── Media ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  @doc("A raster or vector image asset, sized in user units.")
  Image     { source: utf8 alt: utf8? caption: utf8? width: f64? height: f64? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("An embedded video asset.")
  Video     {
    source: utf8 poster: utf8? title: utf8? width: f64? height: f64?
    caption: utf8? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>?
  }
  @doc("A file shipped with the output and offered for download.")
  File      { path: utf8 label: utf8? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("A block equation, carried as LaTeX for the backend to typeset.")
  Math      { latex: utf8 display: bool? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("A page-level drawing: the SVG shape vocabulary, not SVG markup.")
  Drawing   {
    shapes: list<Svg> width: f64? height: f64? caption: utf8? desc: utf8?
    id: identifier? class: list<utf8>?
  }
  @doc("A terminal session: the resolved screen, one string per line.")
  Terminal  { lines: list<utf8> title: utf8? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }

  // ── Apparatus ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  @doc("A table of contents: resolved entries, not a marker for the backend to expand.")
  Toc       { entries: list<ContentTocEntry> title: utf8? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("The footnote apparatus at the foot of a page.")
  Footnotes { notes: list<ContentFootnote> title: utf8? id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  @doc("A chapter's opening header: kicker, title, and the meta line under it.")
  ChapterHeader {
    title: utf8 kicker: utf8? subtitle: utf8? reading_time: utf8? updated: utf8? version: utf8?
    id: identifier? class: list<utf8>?
  }

  // ── Presentation ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  @doc("A step-revealed group on a slide; shown at once outside a deck.")
  Fragment     { body: list<Content> id: identifier? class: list<utf8>? }
  // No `class`: presenter commentary is never part of the rendered page,
  // so it carries no styling hook — exactly as the `notes` block declares
  // none today. (It also keeps this variant's shape distinct from
  // `Fragment`'s, which bare-record dispatch requires.)
  @doc("Presenter-only commentary on a slide; omitted from printed output.")
  SpeakerNotes { body: list<Content> id: identifier? }
}
